INT. UNDERWATER RESTAURANT - DINING ROOM - NIGHT

A glass-walled dining room sunk forty feet beneath a harbor. Green water presses at the panes, and small silver fish dart past candlelit tables. Every sound is muffled, like conversation in a cathedral. A single table is occupied.

LEOPOLDINA "POLLY" ASHWORTH-DIAZ, sixty, in a velvet turban and enough pearls to flood the room, presides at the head of the table. Her nephew CALVIN ASHWORTH, thirties, damp-palmed and rumpled, sits at her left. At her right is her lawyer, ISADORA MCFEE, fifties, sharp as a letter opener, with a briefcase on her knees.

POLLY
Calvin, darling, you've not touched your lobster.

CALVIN
I'm not very hungry, Aunt Polly.

POLLY
Nonsense. You've been hungry since the day you were born.

ISADORA
Miss Ashworth-Diaz, shall we turn to the reason we're here?

POLLY
Isadora, we are here for the lobster.

ISADORA
We are here for the will.

POLLY
We are here for both.

Calvin looks at the water. A fish stares back at him, then wheels away.

CALVIN
I've never liked being this far down.

POLLY
That's the charm of it. Nobody can leave in a huff.

ISADORA
You can leave in a rush, Calvin. There's a lift.

CALVIN
I'll stay.

POLLY
Good boy.

Polly tops off her own wine from the decanter and sips.

POLLY (CONT'D)
Now. Here is how this will go. I have written a will. It names one heir. I will tell you the name over dessert.

CALVIN
Over dessert?

POLLY
The soufflé is divine.

ISADORA
This is irregular.

POLLY
So is my taste in nephews.

Calvin lets out a nervous laugh and stops when nobody joins him. He picks up his fork, studies the lobster, puts it down.

CALVIN
Aunt Polly, if it's me, I want you to know I'd be grateful.

POLLY
And if it isn't?

CALVIN
(a pause)
I'd still be grateful.

POLLY
That is the first honest thing you've said in ten years.

ISADORA
Miss Ashworth-Diaz, I really must insist.

POLLY
Insist all you like, dear. The soufflé takes twenty minutes.

Isadora opens her briefcase, withdraws a thick envelope, and sets it on the white tablecloth. The candle gutters in the stillness.

ISADORA
I have here the sealed document. I am obliged to read it in the presence of the heir.

POLLY
Then you are obliged to wait.

ISADORA
Polly.

POLLY
Isadora.

They hold each other's eyes. Calvin looks from one to the other, as a man might watch a tennis match from inside a fishbowl. Above them, the harbor lights ripple.

A low groan comes from the hull. The glass seems to flex. A thin trickle of water runs down the west wall, and Polly's smile holds.

CALVIN
Is that supposed to happen?

POLLY
The trickle? Oh, yes. It's decorative.

ISADORA
It's not decorative.

POLLY
Well, it is now.

The trickle widens. The candle flames lean. Calvin half rises from his chair.

CALVIN
We should go.

POLLY
Sit down, Calvin.

CALVIN
Aunt Polly, the wall is leaking.

POLLY
The wall is performing.

ISADORA
(standing)
This is absurd. Calvin, we are going.

POLLY
Isadora, sit.

Isadora doesn't sit. A seam of glass cracks, a hair-thin line from floor to ceiling. The sound is small and very precise.

CALVIN
(in a whisper)
Oh no.

POLLY
(calm)
Oh dear. Well. That is a new development.

The line widens. Water jets through it, flat and white. Polly pushes back her chair, finally, and stands.

POLLY (CONT'D)
Calvin. The envelope.

CALVIN
What about it?

POLLY
Take it. Open it. Now.

He seizes it, tears the flap, and pulls out a single sheet. The water is at their ankles. He reads, and his mouth falls open.

CALVIN
It says...

POLLY
Read it aloud, darling.

CALVIN
"To my nephew Calvin I leave nothing. To my lawyer, Isadora McFee, I leave everything, for she has been faithful."

Isadora stares at him. Polly looks at Isadora with bright, fond eyes.

POLLY
Well. Nobody ever tells you the plot twist will happen at forty feet.

ISADORA
I didn't know.

POLLY
Of course you didn't. That's what made it so lovely.

The glass gives way. The sea rushes in, and the candles go out one by one, and the last thing we see is Polly lifting her wine to the dark and saying, to nobody in particular:

POLLY (CONT'D)
Cheers.
